# Machine agent framework (dsh)

The registry at `{{REPO_DIR}}/roles.config.json` is the single source of truth
for every agent profile on this machine. Each profile is installed here as an
`agent-<key>` skill, discovered through dsh's filesystem skill provider at the
`user-dsh` root (`$DSH_HOME/skills`).

{{ROSTER_SENTENCE}}

{{ROSTER_TABLE}}

## What dsh can and cannot do with this framework

**Can:** adopt any profile (load its `agent-<key>` skill and work inside it),
run the deterministic router, delegate with the `subagent` / `subagent_fork`
tools, and run a bounded goal loop.

**Cannot: route a profile to its configured model.** dsh dispatches DeepSeek
models; every profile in this registry is configured on a provider dsh cannot
reach. A profile you adopt runs on **your session's model**. Say that plainly if
asked — never report that a profile ran on its configured model.

A `subagent` child inherits this session's model. Per-profile model routing
would need a separate `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent` instance per profile in
`$DSH_HOME/profiles/<profile>/cordis.patch.yml` with an `agentOptions.model`,
and the values it accepts are DeepSeek models — not the classes in this
registry. Delegation here buys context isolation, not model routing.

The `tools:` list in each profile is Claude Code's tool vocabulary. dsh enforces
its own sandbox and approval presets instead, so a read-only profile's boundary
is a discipline here. Keep it anyway.

**`AGENTS.md` still reaches you.** dsh's `agent-instructions` plugin loads
`$DSH_HOME/AGENTS.md` — a symlink to `/home/dyadmin/AGENTS.md`, the machine
contract — before any project file. If that contract tells you to delegate to a
subagent profile, the profile it means is the corresponding `agent-<key>` skill
here, adopted in-session.

## Picking a profile

- **`runner`** is the everyday front door for routine work, maintenance, and triage.
- **`planner` → `builder`** is the development core for substantive work.
- **`code-reviewer`** runs before every PR. The Git Workflow Standard makes this
  mandatory on every harness. On dsh, adopt `agent-code-reviewer` — ideally in a
  `subagent` child so the review reads the diff with fresh context — and never
  write "reviewed" into a PR body unless a review actually ran.
- Direct-call-only profiles ({{DIRECT_CALL_ONLY}}) run only when the user asks
  for them by name.

